8. Deadlock:
    Thread 1 holds Lock A and waits for Lock B
    Thread 2 holds Lock B and waits for Lock A
   How to solve:
    1) Use consistent lock ordering, always acquire locks in the same order.
    2) Use reentrantLock.tryLock() with timeout, so threads don't wait forever and can back off and retry.

9. Threads communication mainly through shared memory. Coordination is achieved using mechanisms like wait(), notify(),
   volatile variables for visibility, and higher-level utilities such as BlockingQueue, Locks, and Futures. In practice,
   we prefer high-level concurrency utilities like BlockingQueue because they are safer and easier to use.

10. Object lock is associated with a specific instance, so each object has its own lock and threads can run concurrently
    on different objects. Class lock is associated with the Class object itself and is shared across all instances, so only
    one thread can execute static synchronized code at a time. Also, static and instance locks are different, so they do not
    block each other.

11. The join() method in java is used to make one thread wait for another thread to finish execution.

12. Thread.yield() is a hint to the scheduler that the current thread is willing to pause and let other threads run.

13. A ThreadPool is a group of reusable threads used to execute multiple tasks, instead of creating a new thread for each task.
    ThreadPool have 1)Fixed Thread Pool, 2)Cached Thread Pool, 3)Single Thread Pool, 4)Scheduled Thread Pool.
    TaskQueue stores tasks that are waiting to be executed when all threads are busy.

14. Java ThreadPool is provided by: java.util.concurrent
    The core interface is ExecutorService

15. You submit tasks to a ThreadPool using ExecutorService methods like: execute(), submit()

16. The main advantage of a ThreadPool is reusing threads to improve performance and control system resources.

17. shutdown() stops accepting new tasks, continues executing already submitted tasks, and waits for tasks to finish.
    shutdownNow() stops accepting new tasks, attempts to interrupt running threads, and returns tasks that were not executed.

18. Atomic classes are classes in java.util.concurrent.atomic that provide thread-safe, lock-free operations on single variables.
    Atimic classes have 1)Basic types: AtomicInteger, AtomicLong, AtomicBoolean 2)Reference type: AtomicReference<T>
    3)Array types: AtomicIntegerArray, AtomicLogArray, AtomicReferenceArray.
    get(): get value, set(): set value, incrementAndGet(): ++value, getAndIncrement() value++, addAndGet(n): add n
    compareAndSet(a, b): CAS operation, getAndSet(v): atomic replace.

19. Concurrent collections are thread-safe data structures provided in java.util.concurrent that allow multiple threads to acces
    and modify data safely without external synchronization.
    1)ConcurrentHashMap, 2)CopyOnWriteArrayList, 3)CopyOnWriteArraySet, 4)BlockingQueue, 5)ConcurrentLinkedQueue,
    6)ConcurrentSkipListMap/Set

20. 1)Intrinsic lock(synchronized): simple and easy to use, automatically releases lock, JVM optimized.
    2)ReentrantLock: can lock/unlock manually, supports tryLock() to avoid deadlock, support timeout, interruptive lock, can be
    fair lock
    3)ReadWriteLock: multiple threads can read simultaneously, and improves performance for read-heavy system.
    4)StampedLock: Optimistic locking, better performance than ReadWriteLock.
    5)CountDownLatch: wait for multiple threads to finish.

